-- title:
--   f(Xᵖ) ≡ f(X)ᵖ mod p for multivariate power series
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a commutative ring and $p$ a prime number, and assume that $c^p - c$ lies in the ideal $p\mathcal{O}$ generated by the image of $p$ in $\mathcal{O}$, for every $c \in \mathcal{O}$; this holds for instance for $\mathcal{O} = \mathbb{Z}$, $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ or $\mathbb{Z}_p$. Let $\sigma$ be an arbitrary index type, with no finiteness assumption, and let $f \in \mathcal{O}[[X_s : s \in \sigma]]$ be a multivariate formal power series. Write $\mathrm{expand}\ p$ for Mathlib's algebra endomorphism of $\mathcal{O}[[X_s]]$ given by the substitution $X_s \mapsto X_s^p$ (applicable since $p \neq 0$), so that $\sum_m a_m X^m \mapsto \sum_m a_m X^{pm}$. The assertion is that $$\mathrm{expand}\ p\, (f) - f^p \in \text{the ideal of } \mathcal{O}[[X_s : s \in \sigma]] \text{ generated by } p,$$ i.e. $f(X^p) \equiv f(X)^p$ modulo $p\,\mathcal{O}[[X]]$. Equivalently, the substitution $X_s \mapsto X_s^p$ is a lift of the Frobenius endomorphism to $\mathcal{O}[[X_s : s \in \sigma]]$.
--
--   The statement says that $f \mapsto f(X^p)$ is a Frobenius lift on a multivariate power series ring over a ring on which the $p$-th power map is the identity modulo $p$; this is precisely the input hypothesis of Dwork's lemma characterising which sequences are the ghost components of a Witt vector. It is used in the construction of Witt vectors over power series rings whose ghost components are prescribed, via [`MvPowerSeries.exists_wittVector_forall_coeff_ghostComponent_eq_logCovector`](thm.html#MvPowerSeries.exists_wittVector_forall_coeff_ghostComponent_eq_logCovector) and [`MvPowerSeries.exists_wittVector_forall_coeff_ghostComponent_eq_of_forall_natCast_mul_coeff_mem`](thm.html#MvPowerSeries.exists_wittVector_forall_coeff_ghostComponent_eq_of_forall_natCast_mul_coeff_mem).
-- source:
